# The Sectors of the Indian Economy: Primary, Secondary, and Tertiary

> Every rupee of national income is earned in a sector: growing,
> making, or serving. Mapping India's output and workforce across the
> three sectors reveals both the economy's shape and its biggest
> structural puzzle.

## 1. Why Classify Economic Activity?

[definition]
Economic activities are grouped into **sectors** by the kind of work
done: the **primary sector** extracts gifts of nature (agriculture,
forestry, fishing, mining and quarrying); the **secondary sector**
transforms them into goods (manufacturing, construction, electricity,
gas and water); the **tertiary sector** provides services that
support producers and consumers (trade, transport, banking, education,
health, administration).
[/definition]

Classification matters because the three sectors grow at different
speeds, employ different skills, and demand different policies. A
policy for farmers — irrigation, minimum support prices, crop
insurance — cannot serve a software park or a steel plant. Modern
accounts sometimes add a **quaternary sector** for knowledge work
(IT services, research, consultancy), but the three-way split remains
the exam standard.

@diagram tree id="sector-tree" layout="tree" aria="The three sectors of the Indian economy with examples"
  "Economic activity" -> "Primary (extractive)"
  "Economic activity" -> "Secondary (manufacturing)"
  "Economic activity" -> "Tertiary (services)"
  "Primary (extractive)" -> "Crops, dairy, forestry, fishing, mining"
  "Secondary (manufacturing)" -> "Textiles, steel, cement, power, construction"
  "Tertiary (services)" -> "Trade, transport, banking, IT, health, education"
@/diagram

## 2. The Primary Sector: Closest to Nature

The primary sector depends on land, water, and weather. In India it
is dominated by agriculture and allied activities — crops, livestock,
forestry, and fishing — together with mining and quarrying.

- Agriculture is largely **monsoon-dependent**: only about half of
  the net sown area is irrigated, so a deficient south-west monsoon
  still moves food prices and farm incomes.
- Holdings are small — the average farm size is roughly **one
  hectare** — which limits mechanisation and keeps productivity low.
- The sector carries a large share of **disguised unemployment**:
  more family members work on a plot than the plot truly needs.
- Mining and quarrying are primary because they *extract* nature's
  stock — coal from Jharia, iron ore from Odisha's Keonjhar belt —
  even though national accounts group them with "industry".

[warning]
A favourite exam trap: in India's national accounts, mining is
reported inside the *industry* block alongside manufacturing,
electricity, and construction — but conceptually it belongs to the
*primary* sector because it does not transform anything; it merely
takes.
[/warning]

## 3. The Secondary Sector: Making Things

The secondary sector — often called the industrial sector — adds form
value by processing raw materials. It covers registered (factory)
manufacturing, unregistered small units, construction, and electricity
generation.

| Component | What it does | Indian anchors |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Manufacturing | Converts inputs into goods | Textiles of Tiruppur, steel of Jamshedpur, autos of Chennai's "Detroit of India" belt |
| Construction | Builds structures — roads, houses, plants | Employs huge casual labour; a barometer of investment |
| Electricity, gas, water | Produces and distributes utilities | Thermal, hydro, solar and wind plants |

The secondary sector matters for the **earnings effect**: factory
jobs historically paid more than farm work, so the share of industry
in output and employment is a key indicator of structural progress.
This is why the Industrial Policy Resolution of **1956** reserved the
"commanding heights" — heavy machinery, mining, steel, power — for
the public sector, creating PSUs such as SAIL, BHEL, and NTPC.

## 4. The Tertiary Sector: Serving the Economy

Services support both the other sectors and final consumers: trade,
hotels, transport and communication, finance and insurance, real
estate, public administration, defence, education, and health. India
is unusual among developing countries in being **services-led** —
exports of software services, business process outsourcing, and
business services generate large foreign-exchange surpluses, and
cities such as Bengaluru, Hyderabad, and Gurugram grew on this base.

[info]
Why does the tertiary sector grow fastest everywhere? As incomes
rise, households spend proportionally more on services — eating out,
banking, travel, schooling, healthcare. Specialisation also deepens:
activities once done inside households or factories (accounting,
repairs, logistics) spin off as purchased services.
[/info]

## 5. Comparing the Shares: Output versus Employment

Here lies India's structural paradox. Using recent National Accounts
and Periodic Labour Force Survey estimates (values approximate):

@chart bar title="Approximate share of GVA and of employment by sector (per cent)" labels:
Primary
Secondary
Tertiary

data:
18
28
54
@/chart

| Sector | Share of GVA (about) | Share of employment (about) | Gap |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Primary (agriculture and allied) | 15-18% | 45% | Employs far more than it earns |
| Secondary (industry) | 27-29% | 25% | Broadly balanced |
| Tertiary (services) | 53-55% | 30% | Earns far more than it employs |

Roughly **half of India's workers produce only a sixth of its
output**. The arithmetic is unavoidable: average productivity per
worker is lowest in agriculture, so meaningful income convergence
requires shifting workers out of farming into higher-productivity
industry and services — the transition economists call *structural
transformation*.

## 6. A Second Axis: Organised versus Unorganised, Public versus Private

Sector of activity and sector of ownership are different questions.
Cross-cut every primary/secondary/tertiary activity with two more
classifications:

- **Organised (formal) sector**: enterprises registered with the
  government; employment terms fixed and regular; workers get
  pensions, provident fund, paid leave. Examples: Indian Railways,
  SAIL, TCS, government schools.
- **Unorganised (informal) sector**: small and scattered units with
  no written contract or job security — farm labour, street vendors,
  home-based piece work. Roughly **nine out of ten Indian workers**
  are informal, which is why schemes such as the e-Shram
  registration drive matter.
- **Public sector**: owned and controlled by government — railways,
  defence production, most power distribution.
- **Private sector**: owned by individuals or companies, run for
  profit — from Kirana stores to Reliance and Infosys.

[important]
A worker can be any combination: a plumber employed by the municipal
corporation is tertiary + public + organised; the same plumber working
alone is tertiary + private + unorganised. Exams often test exactly
this distinction.
[/important]

## 7. How the Mix Has Shifted Since 1951

At Independence the primary sector produced **more than half of
India's GDP** (about 52-55% in 1950-51) and employed about seven in
ten workers. Since then, every successive decade has seen
agriculture's output share fall — to roughly 42% by 1970-71, near 29%
by 1990-91, and 15-18% today — while the services share climbed from
about a third to well over half.

Two features of this transition deserve note:

1. **Services overtook industry**: unlike East Asian economies, whose
   manufacturing share rose to 30-40% before services dominated,
   India's manufacturing share of GDP has hovered around 15-17% for
   decades. India, in effect, "skipped" the mass-manufacturing stage —
   a pattern sometimes called premature tertiarisation.
2. **Employment lags output**: workers left farming much more slowly
   than output left farming, which is why the productivity gap in
   section 5 persists. Programmes such as Make in India, PLI schemes,
   and MGNREGA are, from different directions, attempts to manage this
   gap.

## 8. Common Confusions and Exam Traps

[warning]
Do not confuse *sector of the economy* (primary/secondary/tertiary)
with *sector of ownership* (public/private) or *formality*
(organised/unorganised). A coal miner in Coal India works in the
primary sector, the public sector, and the organised sector — all at
once.
[/warning]

[tip]
Quick tests: Does the activity take from nature? Primary. Does it
make or build something tangible? Secondary. Does it help producers
or serve consumers without making a physical good? Tertiary. Mining
= primary (extraction), even though it sits in the "industry" block
of national accounts; a tailor stitching at home is secondary; a
teacher is tertiary.
[/tip]

[definition]
MSME size classes (revised 2020) — one more layer of classification
used in policy: **micro** (investment up to ₹1 crore, turnover up to
₹5 crore), **small** (up to ₹10 crore, ₹50 crore), **medium** (up to
₹50 crore, ₹250 crore).
[/definition]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Three sectors | Primary (extractive), secondary (manufacturing), tertiary (services) |
| Mining belongs to | Primary sector (though shown under "industry" in accounts) |
| Largest share of GVA today | Tertiary, about 53-55% |
| Largest share of employment | Primary, about 45% |
| 1950-51 position | Agriculture over half of GDP |
| India's growth pattern | Services-led; manufacturing share stuck near 15-17% |
| Organised vs unorganised | Registered with security of service vs informal, no security |
| Public vs private | Government ownership vs private ownership |
| Key structural task | Shift surplus labour from farms to productive non-farm jobs |
